  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/18-toothcarps-killis-and-livebearers/rivulus-punctatus/",
      "html": "<p>This charming dwarf killi becomes only 3-4 cm long. It is the species of Rivulus found furthest south. The males can have very different patterns in the caudal fin. It belongs to the subgenus Melanorivulus.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/18-toothcarps-killis-and-livebearers/rivulus-punctatus/",
      "html": "<p>In the wild the fish wander through wet grasses and can often be found far from open water.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "maintenance",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/18-toothcarps-killis-and-livebearers/rivulus-punctatus/",
      "html": "<p>Keeping and breeding Rivulus punctatus is rather easy. These fish are excellent jumpers, which often costs them their lives, so the tank must be covered absolutely tightly.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/18-toothcarps-killis-and-livebearers/rivulus-punctatus/",
      "html": "<p>R. punctatus can tolerate temperatures as low as 12°C for a short time, similar to the temperatures a freshwater fish has to face temporarily in these southern parts of South America.</p>"
    }
  ],
